Patterned bruising/burns, Developmentally incompatible history, Multiple healing fractures

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-006
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of patterned bruising and a developmentally incompatible injury mechanism requiring urgent medical assessment and safeguarding intervention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (child), parent, and a visiting community nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported by the community nurse indicated an 18-month-old child with new, patterned bruising and a history from the caregiver that did not match the child's developmental stage. Assessment could not be safely delayed until regular clinic hours due to the immediate need to physically assess for occult trauma and initiate statutory child protection protocols.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Parent states: "He just falls over all the time because he's clumsy."
History of Present Illness: The community nurse requested urgent review during a scheduled welfare check. The parent reports the child sustained bruises to the back and torso after a fall from a standing height yesterday. The history provided by the caregiver is developmentally incompatible with the child's age or abilities, as the child is not yet independently walking. There is an unexplained delay in seeking medical evaluation for what the parent describes as a significant fall. The child displays extreme withdrawal and inappropriate fear of adults during the consultation.
Functional Status: Developmentally delayed for 18 months; not independently ambulating.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate threat of ongoing physical abuse; profound risk of serious morbidity or mortality if left in the current environment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 | HR: 130 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 28 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 9.5 Kg (Below 5th percentile)
General: Withdrawn, avoids eye contact, poorly kempt, visible signs of chronic malnutrition.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Patterned bruising reflecting the shape of an object (linear marks) on the posterior thorax. Multiple ecchymoses in various stages of healing on the abdomen and proximal extremities. Musculoskeletal: Tenderness on palpation of the left lateral ribs. The presentation is highly suspicious for patterned bruising/burns, a developmentally incompatible history, and potentially multiple healing fractures.
Environmental Observations: Residence is cluttered; visible safety hazards present; parent appears agitated and defensive during the exam.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; immunizations are reported as incomplete.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Child Abuse (Non-Accidental Trauma) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Coagulopathy (e.g., Hemophilia, von Willebrand disease) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bruising pattern is strictly mechanical and patterned, rather than diffuse or petechial, and localized to atypical areas for accidental childhood trauma.

Osteogenesis Imperfecta - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of blue sclerae, normal dentition, and the presence of patterned soft tissue injuries incongruent with simple bone fragility.

Accidental Trauma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Mechanism of injury provided (falling from standing) cannot account for the severity, location (torso/back), and varied age of the multiple lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with patterned bruising and focal rib tenderness. The history provided by the caregiver was starkly incompatible with the child's developmental capabilities and the nature of the injuries. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was mandatory to physically examine the child, document the injuries, and immediately initiate mandatory reporting procedures. Delaying this assessment posed an imminent and severe risk to the child's life and safety, fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate physical examination and meticulous documentation of all lesions. Facilitated urgent transfer to the pediatric emergency department via ambulance for a full skeletal survey to assess for occult fractures and formal forensic pediatric evaluation.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: The community nurse will remain with the child continuously during the transfer to the hospital. This ensures immediate physical safety, rewarding the child with a secure presence and preventing further harm.
Step 2: The parent will travel separately to the hospital to meet with social workers. This physically separates the suspected perpetrator from the child, immediately de-escalating the immediate threat environment.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory immediate notification to Child Protection Services.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover completed directly to the Pediatric Emergency physician and hospital social worker.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer to the pediatric emergency department has been arranged. The child is not to remain in the home environment.

